{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Between a man and his wife, between a father and his daughter.</b> The Rabbis of blessed memory in the Sifre made a hermeneutical analogy (היקשא) from this verse: just as a husband annuls only matters that are between him and her, and matters that involve affliction of soul, so too a father annuls only matters that are between him and her, and matters that involve affliction of soul. Matters that are between him and her in the case of a daughter — that is, if she vowed not to attend to him as is proper — and so it is explicit in the Yerushalmi: 'Every vow and every oath of binding to afflict the soul of a woman — her husband shall establish it and her husband shall annul it.' I have only vows that involve affliction of soul; from where do I derive matters that are between him and her? Scripture says: ״between a man and his wife״ — this far concerns the husband. From where concerning the father? Just as the husband annuls only vows that involve affliction of soul and matters that are between him and her, so too the father annuls only vows that involve affliction of soul and matters that are between him and her. This far in the Yerushalmi. And so is the opinion of the Ramban, of blessed memory, in the laws of vows that he composed." "<b>But</b> the Rambam, of blessed memory, did not rely on this, and ruled that a father annuls for his daughter in her youth all her vows and prohibitions according to the plain sense of the verse — just as one does not apply to her 'matters that are between him and her' except in the case of a husband, so too one does not apply to her 'vows of affliction of soul.' And they said that the sages of Lunel seized him on this during his lifetime. One who vows vows in order to direct his intentions and to rectify his deeds — this is seemly and praiseworthy. How so? One who was a glutton and forbade meat upon himself for a year or two; and one who was negligent with wine and forbade wine upon himself for a long time or all the days of his life, in the manner of a Nazirite; and so one who pursued gifts and hastened after wealth and forbade upon himself gifts or the enjoyments of the people of a certain province; and so one who was arrogant and domineering and made vows upon himself — all of them are in the way of the service of the Holy Name, may He be blessed; and regarding such cases the Rabbis of blessed memory said: נדרים סיג לפרישות — vows are a fence for abstinence. Thus wrote the Rambam, of blessed memory. And likewise one swears to bestir oneself for the sake of a commandment when it comes to hand, so that one will not be slack in it, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 119:106): נשבעתי ואקימה לשמור משפטי צדקך, and as it is written (Genesis 28:20): וידר יעקב נדר לאמר — and the Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: what is לאמר — 'to say'? To say to the generations, that they should vow in a time of their distress; and it is written (Numbers 21): וידר ישראל."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:17.054469461Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}